🔬 Key Features
Runtime workload classification
Hardware performance counter monitoring
IPC-based workload characterization
Cache-miss-rate analysis
Compute / memory / mixed workload detection
Adaptive CPU affinity management
Dynamic task migration
Load-aware multicore balancing
Priority-aware workload placement
Migration hysteresis for scheduling stability
Continuous runtime reprofiling
Userspace implementation with no kernel modifications
🧠 Hardware-Aware Workload Classification

The scheduler uses two primary runtime metrics:

Instructions Per Cycle
IPC = Instructions Executed / CPU Cycles

Higher IPC generally indicates compute-intensive execution.

Cache Miss Rate
Miss Rate = Cache Misses / Instructions

Higher cache miss rates indicate increased memory-access pressure.

The scheduler uses these measurements to classify workloads as:

COMPUTE
MEMORY
MIXED

The classification is continuously updated as workload behavior changes.

🖥️ Target Platform
Component	Configuration
Platform	Raspberry Pi 3 Model B
CPU	Quad-core ARM Cortex-A53
CPU Frequency	1.2 GHz
OS	Ubuntu Linux
Scheduler	Userspace daemon
Profiling	Linux perf_event_open()
CPU Control	sched_setaffinity()

The implementation was evaluated using synthetic compute-intensive, memory-intensive, mixed, and dynamically shifting workloads.

📊 Results
System Performance
Metric	Linux CFS	Proposed Scheduler
System IPC	0.59	0.90
Compute IPC	0.70–1.00	0.896–1.045
Memory Miss Rate	13.69%	7.81–7.85%
The measured system IPC increased from 0.59 to 0.90, corresponding to approximately 52% throughput improvement under the evaluated mixed-workload conditions.
🔄 Adaptive Runtime Migration

The scheduler continuously reprofiles workloads.

When a workload transitions between execution characteristics:

COMPUTE
   │
   │ workload phase changes
   ▼
MEMORY
   │
   ▼
Reclassification
   │
   ▼
CPU affinity update
   │
   ▼
Task migration
Migration hysteresis prevents unnecessary oscillation caused by short-lived workload fluctuations.
📈 Core Placement

The scheduler separates workloads according to runtime behavior and priority:

Workload	Priority	Assigned Core
Compute-intensive	High	Core 0
Memory-intensive	High	Core 1
Compute-intensive	Normal	Core 2
Memory-intensive	Normal	Core 3
Mixed / Dynamic	Adaptive	Runtime Migration

This reduces competing workloads sharing the same hardware resources and enables adaptive placement when workload behavior changes.

